// The Frost half of the runtime. Everything a program calls that is not the
// program is here, except what genuinely cannot be written in Frost, which
// stays in `frost_runtime.c` beside this and says why.
//
// Each function here is written as `extern fn` with a body, which is what makes
// it keep the name it was written under: emitted code calls
// `frost_rt_check_index` by that name, and a function the compiler named would
// answer to nothing. See 12.5a of the reference.
//
// That holds for the helpers below as well as for what a program calls, and it
// is why they carry the runtime's prefix rather than a short name. This object
// is linked into every program, so a function here named `fault` would be a
// symbol named `fault` in every program that links it, and the next program to
// write one of its own would not link. `frost_rt_` is the name space that
// cannot be written outside this file, which is what makes it the one to use.
//
// Two rules hold everything in this file:
//
//   - Nothing here may need what it provides. A bounds check that indexes
//     something calls itself, so nothing below indexes an array or a slice, and
//     a number is written a digit at a time rather than through a buffer.
//   - What it bottoms out in is the C stub, declared below. Those declarations
//     are the whole of this file's contact with C.

// Writing a fault out, and ending the process once it is written. Both are in
// the stub: the first because it is stdio, the second because it prints a
// backtrace the platform has to be asked for and then aborts, so a debugger and
// a core file see the failure rather than a tidy exit.
//
// `frost_rt_error` reads to a terminator, so it keeps the gate. What is handed
// to it below is a literal in this file, which has one.
frost_rt_error :: extern fn(text: ^i8)
frost_rt_error_char :: safe extern fn(byte: i64)
frost_rt_stop :: safe extern fn()

// The C library, for the four things below that are a call to it and a
// judgement about what comes back. Declared here rather than reached through
// the stub, because a C function that wraps a C function is a line of C this
// file exists to remove.
malloc :: extern fn(size: i64) -> ^u8
memcpy :: extern fn(destination: ^u8, source: ^u8, count: i64) -> ^u8
memset :: extern fn(destination: ^u8, value: i32, count: i64) -> ^u8
remove :: extern fn(path: ^i8) -> i32

// A piece of a fault's message. The call is gated on the terminator and every
// caller here hands it a literal, so the gate is answered for once.
frost_rt_fault :: extern fn(text: ^i8) {
    unsafe { frost_rt_error(text) }
}

// The digits of a number at or below zero, most significant first.
//
// Counting down from zero rather than up from a positive value is what lets the
// smallest i64 be written at all: its negation does not fit the type, so a
// formatter that starts by taking the absolute value traps on the one number it
// is asked about least often and most needs to say.
frost_rt_fault_digits :: extern fn(value: i64) {
    if (value <= -10) {
        frost_rt_fault_digits(value / 10)
    }
    frost_rt_error_char(48 - (value % 10))
}

// A number in a fault's message. No buffer, because a buffer is indexed and an
// index is what this file is here to check.
frost_rt_fault_int :: extern fn(value: i64) {
    if (value < 0) {
        // 45 is '-'.
        frost_rt_error_char(45)
        frost_rt_fault_digits(value)
        return
    }
    frost_rt_fault_digits(0 - value)
}

// An index against the run it reaches into, answering with the index so an
// access checks inline: `data[frost_rt_check_index(i, len)]`.
//
// One comparison would do it in C, where a negative index read as unsigned is
// enormous. Two say it here, because Frost has the comparison it means and a
// reader should not have to know the cast to see that a negative index is
// caught.
frost_rt_check_index :: extern fn(index: i64, length: i64) -> i64 {
    frost_rt_bounds_check(index, length)
    index
}

// The same question asked where nothing is answered with: the emitted code for
// a write through an index calls this and then does the write.
frost_rt_bounds_check :: extern fn(index: i64, length: i64) {
    if (index < 0 || index >= length) {
        frost_rt_fault("frost: index ")
        frost_rt_fault_int(index)
        frost_rt_fault(" out of bounds for length ")
        frost_rt_fault_int(length)
        frost_rt_fault("\n")
        frost_rt_stop()
    }
}

// A slice's length, answered for where the slice is built rather than at each
// access: `slice_from` is the one place a slice comes from, and a length is
// settled once while an access happens in a loop.
//
// Trusting the pointer and the run behind it is what makes the primitive
// unchecked. A negative count is not an unverifiable claim but a meaningless
// one, so it is refused here.
frost_rt_check_length :: extern fn(length: i64) -> i64 {
    if (length < 0) {
        frost_rt_fault("frost: a slice cannot be ")
        frost_rt_fault_int(length)
        frost_rt_fault(" elements long\n")
        frost_rt_stop()
    }
    length
}

// A view of `count` elements starting `from` elements into a run of `room`,
// refused where it would reach past the end.
//
// This is the part of `slice_from`'s claim that a sub-slice does know. Handed a
// raw pointer, nothing can say how many elements live behind it. Handed a slice,
// the run is right there, so a prefix or a range longer than what is left is not
// an unverifiable claim but a false one: the view that came back carried a
// length the storage did not have, and every access through it was
// bounds-checked against that length and passed.
frost_rt_check_span :: extern fn(from: i64, count: i64, room: i64) -> i64 {
    if (from < 0 || from > room) {
        frost_rt_fault("frost: a view cannot start ")
        frost_rt_fault_int(from)
        frost_rt_fault(" elements into a run of ")
        frost_rt_fault_int(room)
        frost_rt_fault("\n")
        frost_rt_stop()
    }
    if (count < 0 || count > room - from) {
        frost_rt_fault("frost: a view of ")
        frost_rt_fault_int(count)
        frost_rt_fault(" elements starting at ")
        frost_rt_fault_int(from)
        frost_rt_fault(" reaches past a run of ")
        frost_rt_fault_int(room)
        frost_rt_fault("\n")
        frost_rt_stop()
    }
    count
}

// How many bytes `count` elements of `width` take, refused where the product
// would wrap.
//
// A wrapped size asks the allocator for fewer bytes than the caller believes it
// is getting. The slice built over that block carries the count the caller
// asked for, so every read past the block's real end is bounds-checked against
// the wrong number and passes. The multiplication is the whole of the failure,
// so it is done here rather than at each container.
frost_rt_check_size :: extern fn(count: i64, width: i64) -> i64 {
    if (count < 0 || width <= 0) {
        frost_rt_fault("frost: cannot allocate ")
        frost_rt_fault_int(count)
        frost_rt_fault(" elements of ")
        frost_rt_fault_int(width)
        frost_rt_fault(" bytes\n")
        frost_rt_stop()
    }
    // The largest i64, divided rather than multiplied, so the question is asked
    // without doing the arithmetic it is about.
    if (count > 9223372036854775807 / width) {
        frost_rt_fault("frost: ")
        frost_rt_fault_int(count)
        frost_rt_fault(" elements of ")
        frost_rt_fault_int(width)
        frost_rt_fault(" bytes is more memory than can be addressed\n")
        frost_rt_stop()
    }
    count * width
}

// Whether a handle still names what it named. The high seven bits of a
// generation are the container the handle came from and the rest is the slot's
// own count, so one comparison catches both a stale handle and a handle from
// somewhere else, and which it was decides what is said.
frost_rt_generation_check :: extern fn(stored: i64, expected: i64) {
    if (stored != expected) {
        if ((stored >> 24) != (expected >> 24)) {
            frost_rt_fault("frost: handle from another container, this one is ")
            frost_rt_fault_int(stored >> 24)
            frost_rt_fault(" and the handle names ")
            frost_rt_fault_int(expected >> 24)
            frost_rt_fault("\n")
        } else {
            frost_rt_fault("frost: stale handle, slot generation ")
            frost_rt_fault_int(stored & 0xffffff)
            frost_rt_fault(" but handle expected ")
            frost_rt_fault_int(expected & 0xffffff)
            frost_rt_fault("\n")
        }
        frost_rt_stop()
    }
}

// A handle against the container it names, answering with the slot. The low 32
// bits are the index and the high 32 the generation, so `slab[handle]` reading a
// released or out-of-range slot aborts rather than seeing whatever took its
// place.
frost_rt_slot :: extern fn(handle: i64, count: i64, generations: ^i64) -> i64 {
    index := handle & 0xffffffff
    generation := handle >> 32
    frost_rt_bounds_check(index, count)
    // The run behind the pointer is the caller's, and the index into it has just
    // been checked against the count the caller gave for it.
    frost_rt_generation_check(unsafe { generations[index] }, generation)
    index
}

// Arithmetic that cannot answer.
//
// An integer operation whose result does not fit the type it is being computed
// at has no right answer, and wrapping is a wrong one that keeps going. Every
// backend detects the condition itself, inline, and calls this only on the
// branch that has already failed, so an operation that fits costs a compare the
// hardware was doing anyway. The number is the contract with the backends.
frost_rt_arith_trap :: extern fn(what: i64) {
    frost_rt_fault("frost: ")
    match what {
        case 0: frost_rt_fault("this addition overflowed")
        case 1: frost_rt_fault("this subtraction overflowed")
        case 2: frost_rt_fault("this multiplication overflowed")
        case 3: frost_rt_fault("division by zero")
        case 4: frost_rt_fault("remainder by zero")
        case 5: frost_rt_fault("this division overflowed")
        case 6: frost_rt_fault("this negation overflowed")
        case 7: frost_rt_fault("this shift moved by more than the width of its type")
        case 8: frost_rt_fault("this arithmetic left the range of its type")
        case _: frost_rt_fault("arithmetic that cannot answer")
    }
    frost_rt_fault("\n")
    frost_rt_stop()
}

// A byte of text, read unsigned.
//
// Reading through `^i8` would sign-extend anything above 127, and a caller
// comparing what comes back against a character would never match on a byte a
// text file is entitled to hold.
frost_rt_byte_at :: extern fn(text: ^i8, index: i64) -> i64 {
    // The run behind the pointer is the caller's, which is what makes reading
    // through it unchecked wherever it is done.
    bytes := unsafe { ptr_cast($u8, text) }
    cast($i64, unsafe { bytes[index] })
}

// A byte written where the caller says. The same claim about the run, made by
// the caller and not checkable here.
frost_rt_byte_set :: extern fn(buffer: ^i8, index: i64, value: i64) {
    unsafe { buffer[index] = cast($i8, value) }
}

// How many bytes a C string holds, counted to its terminator. The terminator is
// the caller's promise, and it is the whole of what bounds this read.
frost_rt_str_len :: extern fn(text: ^i8) -> i64 {
    var length: i64 = 0
    while (unsafe { text[length] } != 0) {
        length = length + 1
    }
    length
}

// An i64 read through a pointer. A `linear` resource passed to an extern
// arrives as a pointer to the moved-in aggregate, and this is the smallest
// terminal consumer that proves the value crossed intact.
frost_rt_read_i64 :: extern fn(data: ^u8) -> i64 {
    unsafe { ptr_cast($i64, data)^ }
}

// Bytes moved, for a container growing its storage or shifting elements, and
// bytes written, for zeroing a freshly built aggregate. Both are the C library's
// and both are unchecked for the same reason: what is behind each pointer is
// the caller's claim.
frost_rt_mem_copy :: extern fn(destination: ^u8, source: ^u8, size: i64) {
    unsafe { memcpy(destination, source, size) }
}

frost_rt_mem_set :: extern fn(destination: ^u8, value: i64, size: i64) {
    unsafe { memset(destination, cast($i32, value), size) }
}

// A file removed, answering with whether it went. `remove` answers 0 for
// success, which is the opposite of what every caller here reads, so the sense
// is turned over once, here.
frost_rt_remove_file :: extern fn(path: ^i8) -> i64 {
    if (unsafe { remove(path) } == 0) {
        return 1
    }
    0
}

// Allocation for the compiler's own arenas. A request that cannot be met stops
// here with a message rather than answering with nothing and leaving the caller
// to write through it.
//
// Frost has no way to say a call ran out of memory: there is no
// `-> ^u8 ! OutOfMemory` on this, and every caller wraps what comes back in a
// slice without looking. A null wrapped in a slice reads as a run of `count`
// elements at address zero, and each access through it is bounds-checked
// against a length that has nothing to do with what was allocated.
frost_rt_alloc :: extern fn(size: i64) -> ^u8 {
    block := unsafe { malloc(size) }
    zero := 0
    if (block == unsafe { ptr_cast($u8, zero) }) {
        frost_rt_fault("frost: out of memory asking for ")
        frost_rt_fault_int(size)
        frost_rt_fault(" bytes\n")
        frost_rt_stop()
    }
    block
}
